What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Whole blood oxygen delivery (VO) is the amount of oxygen that is transported to the tissues of the body per unit time. It is calculated using the following formula:

VO = VOO × V̇O₂
where:
- VOO is the volume of oxygen in the inspired air (ml/min)
- V̇O₂ is the rate of oxygen uptake (ml/min)

This formula is derived from the Fick equation, which relates the rate of oxygen uptake to the partial pressure of oxygen in the inspired air, the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveolar air, and the rate of blood flow to the lungs. The Fick equation is given by:

V̇O₂ = (VO × (POi - P_aO₂) / (V̇O₂ × (POi - P_aO₂))

In this equation, POi is the partial pressure of oxygen in the inspired air, and P_aO₂ is the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveolar air. V̇O₂ is the rate of oxygen uptake, and V̇O₂ is the volume of blood in the lungs per minute.

To calculate VOO, you need to know the partial pressure of oxygen in the inspired air (POi) and the volume of air that is inhaled per minute (V̇I). The partial pressure of oxygen in the inspired air is usually around 21% (21 kPa), and the volume of air inhaled per minute can be calculated using the following equation:

V̇I = V̇I × (1 - V_d)

In this equation, V̇I is the volume of air inhaled per minute, and V_d is the dead space volume (the volume of air that is not transferred to the alveolar air). The dead space volume is usually around 100 ml.

To calculate V̇O₂, you need to know the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveolar air (P_aO₂) and the rate of blood flow to the lungs (V̇O₂). The partial pressure of oxygen in the alveolar air can be calculated using the following equation:

P_aO₂ = (0.7 × POi) - (0.7 × P_CO₂)

In this equation, P_CO₂ is the partial pressure of carbon dioxide in the alveolar air. The rate of blood flow to the lungs can be calculated using the following equation:

V̇O₂ = V̇I × (1 - V_d) / (V̇I - V_d)

Once you have calculated VOO and V̇O₂, you can use the formula above to calculate the whole blood oxygen delivery (VO).
