<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) for the electrochemical reaction, we first need to determine the overall cell potential (E°cell) for the reaction. The cell potential can be calculated using the standard reduction potentials of the two half-reactions:

E°cell = E°(cathode) - E°(anode)

In this case, Cu2+ is being reduced to Cu, so the cathode is Cu2+/Cu, and Zn is being oxidized to Zn2+, so the anode is Zn/Zn2+.

E°cell = E°(Cu2+|Cu) - E°(Zn2+|Zn)
E°cell = (+0.34 V) - (-0.76 V)
E°cell = +1.10 V

Now that we have the cell potential, we can calculate the Gibbs free energy change using the following equation:

ΔG° = -nFE°cell

where n is the number of moles of electrons transferred in the reaction, F is the Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), and E°cell is the cell potential.

In this reaction, 2 moles of electrons are transferred (Zn loses 2 electrons, and Cu2+ gains 2 electrons).

ΔG° = -(2 mol)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V)
ΔG° = -212,467 J/mol

The Gibbs free energy change for the electrochemical reaction is -212,467 J/mol.
