","Isotopologues and isotopomers are two terms used in chemistry to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. The key difference lies in whether the isotopic substitution affects the molecular structure:

1. **Isotopologues**: These are molecules that have the same molecular structure but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_2 \) (methylenediacetal) can have \( ^1\text{H} \) or \( ^2\text{H} \) (deuterium) atoms, leading to isotopologues like \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_2\text{O}_2 \). The structure remains the same, but the molecular weight varies due to the isotopic substitution.

2. **Isotomers**: These are molecules that have the same molecular formula but different connectivity of atoms due to isotopic substitution. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_4 \) (ethene) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_4 \) (decauterpene) are isotomers because the carbon-hydrogen bonds are replaced by carbon-deuterium bonds, leading to a different molecular structure.

Thus, the key difference is that isotopologues have the same structure but different isotopic compositions, while isotomers have different structures due to isotopic substitution.
