"According to the third law of thermodynamics, perfect crystals at absolute zero have an entropy of zero. What does this law imply about the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
According to the third law of thermodynamics, perfect crystals at absolute zero have an entropy of zero. What does this law imply about the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The third law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of a perfectly-ordered crystal approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero (0 K). This means that the entropy of a perfectly ordered crystal at absolute zero is zero because there are no thermal energy fluctuations to cause disorder.

However, the third law implies that the entropy of an imperfect crystal at absolute zero will be non-zero because it contains disorder. This disorder can be due to impurities, defects, or other irregularities in the crystal lattice. These imperfections prevent the crystal from being perfectly ordered, and thus, it will have some residual entropy even at absolute zero.

In summary, the third law implies that the entropy of an imperfect crystal at absolute zero will be greater than zero, while the entropy of a perfect crystal at absolute zero is exactly zero. $\boxed{\text{The entropy of an imperfect crystal at absolute zero is not zero.}}$
