"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbitals** have electron density concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a central, head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a symmetrical electron distribution.  
- **π molecular orbitals** have electron density concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a side-by-side overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a directional electron distribution.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-on overlap (head-on-head), while π orbitals are formed by side-by-side overlap.  

\boxed{\text{σ: electron density along the axis, π: electron density above/below the axis}}
