3. Set up a series of water samples with the methylene blue solution and the activated carbon samples.
4. Adjust the pH of the water samples to different levels (e.g., 2, 4, 6, 8).
5. Maintain the water samples at different temperatures (e.g., 25°C, 30°C, 40°C).
6. Allow the water samples to come into contact with the activated carbon for different time intervals (e.g., 10, 20, 30, 40 minutes).
7. After the contact time, filter the water samples to separate the activated carbon.
8. Measure the concentration of methylene blue in the filtered water samples using a spectrophotometer or other suitable method.
9. Calculate the percentage removal of methylene blue using the following formula:

