To find the standard enthalpy change (ΔH) for this reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔH = ΔHf(FeCl4)2- + 6ΔHhydr(H2O) - [ΔHhydr([Fe(H2O)6]2+) + 2ΔHhydr(Cl-)]

where ΔHf(FeCl4)2- is the enthalpy of formation for [FeCl4]2-, ΔHhydr(H2O) is the enthalpy of hydration for water (which is approximately -285 kJ/mol), and ΔHhydr([Fe(H2O)6]2+) and ΔHhydr(Cl-) are the enthalpies of hydration for [Fe(H2O)6]2+ and Cl-, respectively.

We are given the following values:

ΔHf(FeCl4)2- = -392 kJ/mol
ΔHhydr(Cl-) = -381 kJ/mol
ΔHhydr(Fe2+) = -340 kJ/mol

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔH = (-392 kJ/mol) + 6(-285 kJ/mol) - [(-340 kJ/mol) + 2(-381 kJ/mol)]

ΔH = -392 kJ/mol - 1710 kJ/mol - (-340 kJ/mol - 762 kJ/mol)

ΔH = -392 kJ/mol - 1710 kJ/mol + 340 kJ/mol + 762 kJ/mol

ΔH = -1700 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between [Fe(H2O)6]2+ and 2Cl- ions to produce [FeCl4]2- complex ion is -1900 kJ/mol.
