Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds = Relative speed in m/s * Time in seconds
Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds = 10 m/s * 25 s
Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds = 250 m

Since the jogger was initially 150 m ahead, we subtract this distance from the total distance covered by the train to find the length of the train.

Length of the train = Distance covered by the train in 25 seconds - Initial distance ahead of the jogger
Length of the train = 250 m - 150 m
Length of the train = 100 m

Therefore, the length of the train is $\boxed{100}$  meters.
