<start_of_turn>model
","The initial energy stored in the capacitor is given by:
\[
W_0 = \frac{1}{2} C V_0^2,
\]
where \( C \) is the capacitance and \( V_0 \) is the initial voltage across the capacitor.  

Given the voltage \( v(t) = 10e^{-4t} \) V, the initial voltage \( V_0 \) is the value of \( v(t) \) at \( t = 0 \):
\[
V_0 = v(0) = 10e^{-4(0)} = 10 \, \text{V}.
\]

The current \( i(t) = 0.2e^{-4t} \) A is given by:
\[
i(t) = C \frac{dv}{dt}.
\]
At \( t = 0 \), the derivative of \( v(t) \) is:
\[
\frac{dv}{dt} = -40e^{-4t} \implies \frac{dv}{dt}(0) = -40 \times 1 = -40 \, \text{V/s}.
\]
Thus, the capacitance \( C \) is:
\[
C = \frac{i(0)}{-40} = \frac{0.2}{40} = 0.005 \, \mu\text{F}.
\]

Substituting \( C = 0.005 \, \mu\text{F} \) and \( V_0 = 10 \, \text{V} \) into the energy formula:
\[
W_0 = \frac{1}{2} \times 0.005 \times 10^6 \times 10^2 = 250 \, \text{J}.
\]

Thus, the initial energy stored in the capacitor is \(\boxed{250 \, \text{J}}\).
