In an elastic scattering process where the electron mass is negligible, let the initial and final four-momenta of the electron be \( P_i = (E_i/c, \mathbf{p}_i) \) and \( P_f = (E_f/c, \mathbf{p}_f) \), respectively. Derive an expression for the squared four-momentum transfer \( Q^2 \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The four-momentum transfer \( Q^2 \) is defined as the difference of the initial and final four-momenta, squared:
\[
Q^2 = (P_i - P_f)^2.
\]
Expanding this, we get:
\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_i}{c} - \frac{E_f}{c} \right)^2 - (\mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{p}_f)^2.
\]
Since \( \mathbf{p}_i = \frac{E_i}{c} \hat{p}_i \) and \( \mathbf{p}_f = \frac{E_f}{c} \hat{p}_f \), we can rewrite the squared four-momentum transfer as:
\[
