The Dufort–Frankel method for the one-dimensional diffusion equation \(\frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} = \kappa \frac{\partial^2 \phi}{\partial x^2}\) is given by:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Show that this method produces stable, non-growing solutions for any \(\Delta t\).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To prove the stability of the Dufort–Frankel method, we analyze the eigenvalues of the operator \(\lambda\) that governs the time evolution of the solution. The eigenvalues are derived from the solution of the operator equation:
\[
\lambda \phi_j^{n+1} = \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
The eigenvalues are found by solving the characteristic equation derived from this equation. The eigenvalues are:
\[
\lambda = 1 - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} (1 - \cos(\pi \Delta x / \Delta t)).
\]
The eigenvalues are stable if their absolute values are less than 1. For the Dufort–Frankel method, the eigenvalues are:
\[
|\lambda| = \left|1 - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} (1 - \cos(\pi \Delta x / \Delta t))\right|.
\]
The eigenvalues are independent of \(\Delta t\) and \(\Delta x\), and their absolute value is less than 1 for all \(\Delta t\). Therefore, the Dufort–Frankel method is stable for any \(\Delta t\). 

\[
\boxed{\text{The Dufort–Frankel method is stable for any } \Delta t.}
