2. The heat lost by the gases is:
   \[
   Q_{\text{gasses}} = m c_p (T_{\text{initial}} - T_{\text{final}}) = 1.005 \, \text{kJ/kgK} \times (1373 - 823) \, \text{K} = 550.6 \, \text{kJ/kg}
   \]

3. The entropy change for the gases is:
   \[
   \Delta S_{\text{gasses}} = \int \frac{dQ}{T} = m c_p \ln \left( \frac{T_{\text{final}}}{T_{\text{initial}}} \right) = 1 \, \text{kg} \times 1.005 \, \text{kJ/kgK} \times \ln \left( \frac{823}{1373} \right) = -0.5506 \, \text{kJ/K}
   \]

For the water:
1. The water evaporates at 220°C (493 K).
2. The heat absorbed by the water is equal to the heat lost by the gases:
   \[
   Q_{\text{water}} = m L = 1 \, \text{kg} \times 1858.5 \, \text{kJ/kg} = 1858.5 \, \text{kJ/kg}
   \]
3. The entropy change for the water is:
   \[
   \Delta S_{\text{water}} = \frac{Q_{\text{water}}}{T} = \frac{1858.5 \, \text{kJ/kg}}{493 \, \text{K}} = 3.60 \, \text{kJ/K}
   \]

The total entropy change for the combined system is:
\[
\Delta S_{\text{total}} = \Delta S_{\text{gasses}} + \Delta S_{\text{water}} = -0.5506 \, \text{kJ/K} + 3.60 \, \text{kJ/K} = 2.8494 \, \text{kJ/K}
\]

The total entropy of the combined system increases by \(\boxed{2.8494 \, \text{kJ/K}}\).

