","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\lambda_f - \lambda_i = \frac{h}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\lambda_f = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\) (final wavelength),
- \(\lambda_i = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\) (initial wavelength),
- \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s}\) (Planck's constant),
- \(m_e = 9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\) (electron mass),
- \(c = 2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\) (speed of light),
- \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (maximum wavelength shift occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\), i.e., the electron recolides back back back).

Substituting the values:
\[
\lambda_f - 2.00 \, \text{Å} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} (1 - \cos 180^\circ)
\]
\[
\lambda_f - 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.35 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}
\]
\[
\lambda_f = 2.00 \, \text{Å} + 2.35 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} = 2.00 \, \text{Å} + 0.235 \, \text{Å} = 2.235 \, \text{Å}
\]

The maximum percentage change is:
\[
\frac{\lambda_f - \lambda_i}{\lambda_i} \times 100 = \frac{2.235 - 2.00}{2.00} \times 100 = \boxed{11.75\%}
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{11.75\%}\).
